Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 21:10:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 052/154] feat: the app menu Settings item opens the Atrium
 window
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Recovered from e155a72b08382 rather than reinvented, then cut down. The
original carried a dylib gate and a degraded path that forwarded to
AppController's showPreferences: when no Swift answered. Neither has a
meaning now - the window is backed by a statically linked core - so the
handler holds no original target and always opens ours.

THE VALIDATION ANSWER, decided rather than inherited. Retargeting stops
AppController's -validateUserInterfaceItem: running for this item, because
AppKit asks the item's explicit target, so an answer has to be given. It is
YES, and not merely because that is simplest: stock gates the item on
canOpenNewBrowser because chrome://settings is a TAB needing a browser window
to host it. This window is its own top-level window, one per process, and
opens from a running app with every browser window closed. The stock condition
is about a constraint this window does not have.

Verified by settings-menu-item, 5 of 5: retargeted at startup, the item
claimed, the window built, the route ready, and two windows with the second
titled 'Atrium Settings'. The click assertion was measured absent on a run
that opened the menu and stopped, so it cannot pass on the menu appearing.
The captured menu also shows the item ENABLED, which is the validation answer
visible on screen.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
